  "learnOtpTitle": "OTP and password requests",
  "learnOtpBody": "Treat requests to share a code with care, even when a sender claims to be support. An ordinary OTP reminder may tell you never to share the code. Verify requests through the official app you opened yourself.",
  "learnBankTitle": "Fake bank and e-wallet alerts",
  "learnBankBody": "An account suspension threat can pressure you to tap a link. Sender names can be spoofed. Open your bank or e-wallet app directly to check your account; do not use the message's link as proof.",
  "learnGovernmentTitle": "Government impersonation",
  "learnGovernmentBody": "Messages may claim to come from SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG, or another agency. Verify unexpected aid, penalty, or registration requests using an independently opened official service. A logo or government name does not prove authenticity.",
  "learnDeliveryTitle": "Unexpected delivery fees",
  "learnDeliveryBody": "A small redelivery fee may lead to a page asking for card or account details. Check the order inside the shopping or courier app. A shortened link hides its destination; Usisa does not expand it.",
  "learnSellerTitle": "Online seller payments",
  "learnSellerBody": "Be cautious about pressure to move a conversation or payment outside a marketplace's normal process. Confirm the seller, item, recipient and payment purpose through trusted records before sending money.",
  "learnRecoveryTitle": "If you already shared details",
  "learnRecoveryBody": "Pause further payments and contact your bank or e-wallet through its official app or a trusted contact from your own records. Change affected passwords through the real service. Keep relevant evidence privately. Never share another OTP with someone promising recovery.",
